# Line endings, deliberately NARROW.
#
# THIS FILE DOES NOT SAY `* text=auto eol=lf`, and that is the whole point of it.
# The agent repo does say that, correctly: it ships a ZIP whose integrity hashes must not
# depend on the seller's OS. This repo is different. Its build copies source files into dist
# byte-for-byte and `npm run verify:live` then asserts that every one of them arrives from
# bugit.dev byte-identical. On 2026-09-21, 103 of the 123 text files live on bugit.dev held
# CRLF. A blanket normalisation would therefore have rewritten 103 published files, turned the
# live-delivery check red until a redeploy, and bought nothing a customer can see.
#
# So only the files that actually caused a failure are pinned. The failure was this: a
# generator writes verify.json with LF, git checks it back out as CRLF because core.autocrlf
# is true here, and a checker that compares the whole file byte-for-byte reports a release as
# stale while every field in it matches. That cost a false red on 2026-09-20 and is the only
# line-ending class git itself is responsible for here. (The other CRLF failures that night
# were mine: literal CR characters written into a regex by a patch script.)
#
# A checker should also not care -- sync-release-identity.mjs now compares values with the
# carriage returns stripped -- but a pin costs nothing and the next checker will not have to
# remember.
*.json  text eol=lf

# Never diffed or normalised, whatever a heuristic thinks. No file's bytes change from this;
# it only removes the chance that git ever decides one of these is text.
*.pdf   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.mp4   binary
*.webm  binary
*.zip   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
